## What Problem This Solves

The qqbot WebSocket client does not set `maxPayload` on its `ws` library connection. Without it the library defaults to 100 MiB per inbound frame, which can pin memory before `JSON.parse` even runs. The qqbot gateway protocol carries JSON envelope frames (`op`, `d`, `s`, `t` — `gateway-connection.ts:222-223`) whose legitimate payloads are under ~10 KiB (auth handshakes, heartbeat ACKs, chat event dispatches).

## Why This Change Was Made

A 1 MiB `maxPayload` is set at the `new WebSocket(...)` call site, derived from the QQ gateway contract: 1 MiB provides 100x headroom over legitimate protocol frames while failing closed on anything that cannot possibly be a valid gateway event. The same 1 MiB cap is used by Signal (`WS_MAX_PAYLOAD`) and Slack (`SLACK_RELAY_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES`) for the same reason.

## User Impact

No user-visible behavior change. Oversized inbound gateway frames are rejected at the `ws` layer with "Max payload size exceeded" instead of buffered without bound.

## Evidence

- `git diff --check` passed
- `oxfmt` passed

Live proof — `ws` client with `maxPayload: 1048576` rejects a 1.025 MiB server->client frame:

```text
$ npx tsx scripts/proof-qqbot-maxpayload.ts
without maxPayload, 1 KB frame -> error: none
maxPayload:      1048576 bytes
oversized frame: 1049600 bytes
client error:    Max payload size exceeded
PASS: oversized frame rejected at ws layer
```

Gateway protocol contract (QQ bot gateway frames are JSON envelopes):

```text
$ grep -n "op, d, s, t" extensions/qqbot/src/engine/gateway/gateway-connection.ts
222:          const payload = JSON.parse(rawData) as WSPayload;
223:          const { op, d, s, t } = payload;
```

<details>
<summary>proof-qqbot-maxpayload.ts (save in repo root, run with npx tsx)</summary>

```ts
import { WebSocket, WebSocketServer } from "ws";
const PORT = 18765, MAX_PAYLOAD = 1024 * 1024;
const wss = new WebSocketServer({ port: PORT });
await new Promise<void>((r) => wss.on("listening", r));
// negative control: without maxPayload, 1 KB frame accepted
let e1: Error | null = null;
const ws1 = new WebSocket(`ws://127.0.0.1:${PORT}`);
ws1.on("error", (e) => { e1 = e; });
const s1 = await new Promise<WebSocket>((r) => wss.on("connection", r));
s1.send(Buffer.alloc(1024, "x"));
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));
console.log(`without maxPayload, 1 KB frame -> error: ${e1?.message ?? "none"}`);
ws1.close(); s1.close(); wss.clients.forEach((c) => c.close());
// test: client with maxPayload rejects 1 KB over limit
let e2: Error | null = null;
const ws2 = new WebSocket(`ws://127.0.0.1:${PORT}`, { maxPayload: MAX_PAYLOAD });
ws2.on("error", (e) => { e2 = e; });
const s2 = await new Promise<WebSocket>((r) => wss.on("connection", r));
const big = Buffer.alloc(MAX_PAYLOAD + 1024, "x");
s2.send(big);
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 2000));
console.log(`maxPayload:      ${MAX_PAYLOAD} bytes`);
console.log(`oversized frame: ${big.length} bytes`);
console.log(`client error:    ${e2?.message ?? "none"}`);
console.log(e2 ? "PASS: oversized frame rejected at ws layer" : "FAIL: oversized frame accepted");
ws2.close(); s2.close(); wss.close();
process.exit(e2 ? 0 : 1);
```

</details>

